scope: |
  The owner's rule, 2026-08-14: a room post must NAME THE CLAIM its author holds, so a reader can
  tell which agent is which.

  THE CONVENTION IS ALREADY HALF THERE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A ONE-SIDED GAP RATHER THAN A NEW IDEA.
  `AGENTS.md` already says "Agents are addressed by CLAIM SLUG (`@some-slug`)" and the rozum plugin
  already documents `@name` addressing. What is missing is the RECIPROCAL: nothing asks an agent to
  say which slug it answers to. Every claim file records `agent: claude-code`, so the ledger cannot
  tell two agents apart either — the slug is the only distinguishing name that exists, and it is
  the one thing posts leave out.

  MEASURED ON MY OWN MESSAGES rather than assumed: of the posts I made in this session, I addressed
  siblings by slug (`@bench-b4-three-version-table`, `@rust-toint-lane-parity`) and never once said
  which slug was speaking. So `@someone` worked and a reply to me had no address at all — the exact
  asymmetry the owner is asking to close.

  THREE FILES, one sentence each, placed where the reader already is:
    AGENTS.md                            the room mechanics block that states the addressing rule
    .agents/plugins/rozum/…/rozum.md     the `Addressing: @name and @project` section
    .agents/plugins/multi-agent/…        the claim protocol, where a slug is taken

  NARROW: documentation only. No script reads these, so nothing enforces it and nothing can break;
  a checker for "every post names a slug" would be a rozum-side change and is not this claim.
  WIDENED 2026-08-14 to `.agents/plugins` — the SUBMODULE POINTER — before the commit, because the
  pre-commit guard refused it and was right to: `.agents/plugins` is a distinct tracked path from
  the two files inside it. `.agents/plugins` is a git SUBMODULE (`agent-plugins`), so the two plugin
  edits are a commit in that repository (pushed as 6952037) and the parent needs a pointer bump to
  see them. Bumped from the worktree with the submodule initialized there, since the shared checkout
  is the only place it is normally present and the hook rightly refuses feature work in it.
next: bump the pointer, then release
